// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ibps-po"] = {"qs": [["English Language", "Rearrange the sentences to form a coherent paragraph.\nP: With a microloan from a self-help group, she finally bought her first machine.\nQ: Meena had always wanted to start a small tailoring business at home.\nR: Within a year, she was earning enough to repay the loan and hire a helper.\nS: However, she lacked the money to buy even a single sewing machine.", ["SQPR", "QPSR", "QSPR", "PQSR"], 2, "Q introduces Meena's wish, S states the obstacle with 'However', P resolves it through the microloan, and R gives the result a year later. Hence QSPR forms the logical sequence."], ["English Language", "Choose the word that best fills the blank.\nEvery bank must ________ with the know-your-customer norms before opening a new account.", ["comply", "compile", "compel", "complain"], 0, "'Comply with' means to act in accordance with a rule, which is what banks must do with KYC norms. Compile, compel and complain are similar-sounding but do not fit with 'with the norms'."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Ravi's salary is first raised by 25% and then the new salary is reduced by 20%. What is the net change in his salary?", ["5% increase", "No change", "5% decrease", "2% increase"], 1, "Net multiplier = 1.25 × 0.80 = 1.00, so there is no change. Simply subtracting 20 from 25 to get a 5% increase is wrong because the 20% cut applies to the larger raised salary."], ["English Language", "Choose the word that best fills the blank.\nThe committee's report was so ________ that even experts found it difficult to follow.", ["lucid", "convoluted", "concise", "candid"], 1, "Convoluted means extremely complex and hard to follow, which matches 'even experts found it difficult'. Lucid and concise mean clear and brief, which contradict the sentence; candid means frank."], ["English Language", "Choose the option that best replaces the part in capitals. If no change is needed, choose 'No improvement required'.\nHe insisted ON TO PAY the bill himself.", ["on paying", "to pay", "for paying", "No improvement required"], 0, "The verb 'insist' takes the preposition 'on' followed by a gerund: 'insisted on paying'. 'Insisted to pay' and 'insisted for paying' are incorrect usages, so the sentence needs improvement."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the curved surface area of a cylinder with radius 7 cm and height 10 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", ["1,540 cm²", "440 cm²", "308 cm²", "880 cm²"], 1, "Curved surface area = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 7 × 10 = 440 cm². 1,540 cm³ would be the volume (πr²h), a common confusion, and 880 cm² doubles the correct value."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nFinancial literacy campaigns often focus on teaching people to save, but saving alone is not enough. A household that keeps all its surplus as cash at home loses purchasing power every year to inflation. Knowing where to save, and understanding the trade-off between safety, liquidity and return, matters as much as the habit itself.\nWhich of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?", ["Inflation harms only those households that save regularly every month", "Saving well needs knowledge of where and how to save, not just the habit", "Financial literacy campaigns have been of no real use to households", "Liquidity is always more important than the safety of one's savings"], 1, "The passage says saving 'alone is not enough' and that knowing where to save and weighing safety, liquidity and return matters as much. It never calls campaigns useless or ranks liquidity above safety."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What approximate value should come in place of the question mark (?)\n39.97% of 1200.05 + 14.98² = ?", ["705", "685", "725", "745"], 0, "Round off: 40% of 1200 = 480 and 15² = 225. So the value is about 480 + 225 = 705. Rounding to the nearest convenient numbers first saves time in the exam."], ["English Language", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains a grammatical error.\nIf I was (A) / in your place, (B) / I would accept (C) / the transfer offer. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 0, "In an imaginary or hypothetical condition, the subjunctive 'were' is used with all subjects: 'If I were in your place'. Hence part (A) contains the error; the rest is correct."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Three fair coins are tossed together. What is the probability of getting at least two heads?", ["1/2", "3/8", "5/8", "1/4"], 0, "Of 8 equally likely outcomes, exactly two heads occur in 3 (HHT, HTH, THH) and three heads in 1. So P = 4/8 = 1/2. 3/8 counts only 'exactly two heads' and misses HHH."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Priya walks 8 m south, turns left and walks 6 m, turns left again and walks 14 m, then turns right and walks 2 m. How far and in which direction is she from her starting point?", ["10 m, North-East", "10 m, South-East", "14 m, North-East", "8 m, North"], 0, "South 8 then left (east) 6, left (north) 14, right (east) 2. Net: 8 m east and 6 m north of the start. Distance = √(8² + 6²) = 10 m, towards the North-East."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In the following question, the relationship between different elements is shown. Decide which conclusion(s) definitely hold true.\nGiven: P ≥ Q = R > S; T ≥ R\nConclusions: I. T > S  II. P > T", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 0, "Combining, T ≥ R > S gives T > S, so I follows. P and T are both only known to be ≥ R, so their relative order cannot be fixed and II does not follow. Only I follows."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nOnce considered a novelty, QR-code payments have become ubiquitous in Indian towns; even roadside tea stalls display a code beside the kettle. Yet this convenience brings a new risk: fraudsters paste their own codes over a merchant's, diverting payments that customers believe they are making to the shop.\nAs used in the passage, the word 'ubiquitous' most nearly means:", ["Very expensive", "Hard to trust", "Rarely noticed", "Found everywhere"], 3, "Ubiquitous means present everywhere. The example of even roadside tea stalls displaying a QR code shows how widespread such payments have become, so 'found everywhere' fits the context."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A trader sells two mobile phones at ₹9,600 each. On one he gains 20% and on the other he loses 20%. What is his overall result?", ["Loss of ₹800", "No profit, no loss", "Gain of ₹800", "Loss of ₹400"], 0, "Cost prices are 9600/1.2 = ₹8,000 and 9600/0.8 = ₹12,000, total ₹20,000. Selling price is ₹19,200, so the loss is ₹800 (4%). Equal gain and loss percentages on equal SPs always mean a loss."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nOnce considered a novelty, QR-code payments have become ubiquitous in Indian towns; even roadside tea stalls display a code beside the kettle. Yet this convenience brings a new risk: fraudsters paste their own codes over a merchant's, diverting payments that customers believe they are making to the shop.\nAccording to the passage, how do fraudsters divert payments meant for merchants?", ["By hacking into the bank accounts of the roadside tea stalls", "By asking customers to pay in cash instead of by QR code", "By covering the merchant's QR code with a code of their own", "By printing fake currency notes and passing them to shopkeepers"], 2, "The passage states that fraudsters paste their own codes over a merchant's, so customers unknowingly pay the fraudster. Hacking accounts or fake notes is not mentioned anywhere in the passage."], ["Reasoning Ability", "P # Q means P is the father of Q; P @ Q means P is the sister of Q; P $ Q means P is the wife of Q.\nIn the expression M $ N # O @ R, how is R related to M?", ["Daughter only", "Son or daughter", "Niece", "Sister"], 1, "M is N's wife and N is O's father, so M is O's mother. O is R's sister, so R is also a child of M. R's gender is not given, so R is M's son or daughter."], ["English Language", "Choose the pair of words that best fills both blanks.\nThe new policy was ________ by the board, but its implementation was ________ due to a shortage of funds.", ["rejected, hastened", "approved, accelerated", "opposed, completed", "approved, delayed"], 3, "'But' signals contrast: the board approved the policy, yet a fund shortage delayed it. A shortage of funds cannot logically accelerate or complete implementation, so the other pairs fail."], ["English Language", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains a grammatical error.\nThe number of customers (A) / visiting the branch (B) / have increased sharply (C) / since last year. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 2, "The subject is 'the number', which is singular, so the verb must be 'has increased'. Contrast 'a number of customers have', where the plural verb would be correct. The error is in part (C)."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statement: A bank announces that customers who register their mobile number will receive an SMS alert for every transaction in their account.\nWhich of the following is implicit in the statement?", ["Customers who do not own mobile phones will close their accounts soon", "SMS alerts will completely eliminate all kinds of banking frauds", "Many customers would like to be informed of transactions in their account", "The bank has never informed its customers about any transaction earlier"], 2, "Offering alerts assumes customers value being informed about their transactions. Claims about closing accounts, ending all fraud, or past bank practice go beyond the statement and are not assumed."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Among J, K, L, M and N, each of a different weight: K is heavier than both M and N but lighter than L. M is heavier than N. J is lighter than N. Who is the lightest?", ["N", "M", "K", "J"], 3, "From the clues, L > K > M > N, and J is lighter than N. So the order is L > K > M > N > J, making J the lightest. N is second-lightest, a tempting trap."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In a code language, 'bank gives loan' is written as 'mo ta zi', 'loan is cheap' as 'zi ka pu' and 'cheap bank rate' as 'pu mo xe'. What is the code for 'gives'?", ["mo", "zi", "ta", "ka"], 2, "'bank' is common to sentences 1 and 3 (mo), 'loan' to 1 and 2 (zi), 'cheap' to 2 and 3 (pu). In sentence 1, the remaining code ta must stand for 'gives'."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "In how many ways can a committee of 3 men and 2 women be formed from 6 men and 5 women?", ["150", "300", "252", "200"], 3, "Choose men and women independently and multiply: 6C3 × 5C2 = 20 × 10 = 200. 252 is 10C5, which ignores the condition of exactly 3 men and 2 women."], ["English Language", "Choose the word that is INCORRECTLY spelt.", ["Embarrass", "Occurrence", "Millenium", "Questionnaire"], 2, "The correct spelling is 'Millennium', with double l and double n. Embarrass (double r, double s), occurrence (double c, double r) and questionnaire (double n) are all spelt correctly."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "P, Q and R invest in a business in the ratio 3 : 4 : 5. P keeps the money for 12 months, Q for 9 months and R for 6 months. Out of an annual profit of ₹34,000, what is R's share?", ["₹12,000", "₹9,000", "₹10,000", "₹8,500"], 2, "Profit is shared in the ratio of capital × time: 36 : 36 : 30 = 6 : 6 : 5. R's share = 5/17 × 34,000 = ₹10,000. Ignoring time and using 3 : 4 : 5 would wrongly favour R."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In a row of students, Kiran is 15th from the left end. When she moves six places to her right, she becomes 22nd from the right end. How many students are there in the row?", ["43", "42", "36", "41"], 1, "After moving six places right, Kiran is 21st from the left and 22nd from the right. Total = 21 + 22 − 1 = 42. Using her original 15th position with 22 would give the wrong answer."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Find the roots of each quadratic below and decide how x relates to y.\nI. x² − 9x + 20 = 0\nII. y² − 11y + 30 = 0", ["x < y", "x > y", "x ≤ y", "Relationship cannot be established"], 2, "I gives x = 4 or 5; II gives y = 5 or 6. Every x is less than or equal to every y, and x = y = 5 is possible, so the relationship is x ≤ y, not strictly x < y."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the compound interest on ₹20,000 for 1 year at 10% per annum, compounded half-yearly?", ["₹2,000", "₹2,100", "₹2,025", "₹2,050"], 3, "Half-yearly compounding gives 5% per half-year for 2 periods. Amount = 20,000 × 1.05² = ₹22,050, so CI = ₹2,050. Simple interest would be ₹2,000, the tempting wrong option."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Five persons P, Q, R, S and T live on different floors of a five-storey building; the ground floor is numbered 1 and the top floor 5.\n• Only two persons live between P and T, and P lives above T.\n• S lives immediately above T.\n• Q lives on an even-numbered floor.\n• R lives below S.\nWho lives on floor 1?", ["T", "Q", "S", "R"], 3, "P above T with two between gives T on 1 or 2. T on 1 forces Q onto an odd floor, so T = 2, S = 3, P = 5. Q must be on 4 (even), leaving R on floor 1."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Pointing to a woman, Karan said, 'Her mother's only son is my father.' How is the woman related to Karan?", ["Aunt", "Sister", "Mother", "Cousin"], 0, "The only son of the woman's mother is the woman's brother, and he is Karan's father. So the woman is the sister of Karan's father, which makes her Karan's aunt (paternal aunt)."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: All accounts are deposits. Some deposits are loans. No loan is a bond.\nConclusions: I. Some accounts are loans. II. Some deposits are not bonds.", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 1, "Accounts lie inside deposits, but the deposits that are loans may lie outside accounts, so I is not certain. The deposits that are loans can never be bonds, so some deposits are definitely not bonds. Only II follows."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In a certain code language, PLANT is written as QNDRY. How is GRAIN written in that code?", ["HTDLS", "HSDMS", "HTCMS", "HTDMS"], 3, "Each letter moves forward by its position: +1, +2, +3, +4, +5 (P→Q, L→N, A→D, N→R, T→Y). Applying the same to GRAIN: G→H, R→T, A→D, I→M, N→S, giving HTDMS."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "In what ratio must rice costing ₹40 per kg be mixed with rice costing ₹55 per kg so that the mixture costs ₹45 per kg?", ["1 : 2", "3 : 2", "2 : 1", "2 : 3"], 2, "By alligation: cheaper : dearer = (55 − 45) : (45 − 40) = 10 : 5 = 2 : 1. Since ₹45 is closer to ₹40, more of the cheaper rice is needed, which rules out 1 : 2."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Two trains 180 m and 120 m long are running in opposite directions on parallel tracks at 54 km/h and 36 km/h. In how many seconds will they completely cross each other?", ["12 seconds", "15 seconds", "20 seconds", "10 seconds"], 0, "In opposite directions speeds add: 90 km/h = 25 m/s. Total length to cover = 180 + 120 = 300 m. Time = 300 ÷ 25 = 12 seconds. Using the difference of speeds would give 60 seconds."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What will come in place of the question mark (?) in the series?\n5, 11, 24, 51, 106, ?", ["212", "215", "221", "217"], 3, "Each term is double the previous term plus an increasing number: ×2+1, ×2+2, ×2+3, ×2+4. Next: 106 × 2 + 5 = 217."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Study the arrangement:\nR 4 % T 7 K @ 2 M 9 # E 5 $ U 3 & B\nHow many numbers in the arrangement are immediately followed by a symbol?", ["Four", "Three", "Two", "Five"], 0, "The number–symbol pairs are 4 %, 9 #, 5 $ and 3 &, so four numbers qualify. 7 is followed by K and 2 by M, so they are excluded."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A boat's speed in still water is 15 km/h and the speed of the stream is 3 km/h. The boat goes 36 km downstream and returns to the start. What is its average speed for the whole journey?", ["15 km/h", "13.6 km/h", "14 km/h", "14.4 km/h"], 3, "Downstream 18 km/h takes 2 h; upstream 12 km/h takes 3 h. Total distance 72 km in 5 h gives 14.4 km/h. It is less than 15 km/h because more time is spent at the slower upstream speed."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: Some cards are coins. No coin is a key.\nConclusions: I. Some cards are not keys. II. All keys being cards is a possibility.", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 2, "The cards that are coins cannot be keys, so I definitely follows. Nothing prevents keys from lying entirely inside the card circle (outside coins), so the possibility in II also holds. Both follow."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Six persons A, B, C, D, E and F sit in a row facing north.\n• C sits third from the left end.\n• Only one person sits between C and F.\n• A sits immediately to the right of F.\n• B sits at one of the extreme ends.\n• D is not an immediate neighbour of C.\nWho sits second from the right end?", ["E", "B", "D", "A"], 2, "F cannot be at position 5 (A must be on its right), so F is 1st and A 2nd. B takes the right end. D cannot be 4th (next to C), so D is 5th and E 4th: F A C E D B."], ["English Language", "Select the synonym of the given word: AMELIORATE", ["Worsen", "Improve", "Ignore", "Complicate"], 1, "To ameliorate is to make something bad or unsatisfactory better, so 'improve' is the synonym. 'Worsen' is its opposite, and ignore or complicate do not carry the sense of making better."], ["English Language", "Pick the antonym of OBSCURE from the options given below.", ["Evident", "Hidden", "Vague", "Murky"], 0, "Obscure means not clear or hard to understand. 'Evident' means plainly visible or clear, making it the antonym. Hidden, vague and murky are close in meaning to obscure, not opposite."], ["English Language", "Choose the correct meaning of the idiom: 'To bite the bullet'.", ["To speak rudely to a senior colleague", "To make a hasty decision without thinking", "To avoid a problem for as long as possible", "To face a painful situation with courage"], 3, "'Bite the bullet' means to accept and face something unpleasant or difficult bravely, for example accepting a tough cost-cutting decision. Avoiding a problem is the opposite of its meaning."], ["English Language", "Choose the option that best replaces the part in capitals. If no change is needed, choose 'No improvement required'.\nScarcely had she reached the station WHEN THE TRAIN LEFT.", ["than the train left", "then the train left", "when the train had left", "No improvement required"], 3, "'Scarcely' pairs with 'when' (just as 'no sooner' pairs with 'than'), and the past perfect belongs in the first clause. So the sentence is already correct; 'than' is a common trap."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Five persons P, Q, R, S and T live on different floors of a five-storey building; the ground floor is numbered 1 and the top floor 5.\n• Only two persons live between P and T, and P lives above T.\n• S lives immediately above T.\n• Q lives on an even-numbered floor.\n• R lives below S.\nHow many persons live between Q and R?", ["One", "Three", "None", "Two"], 3, "The arrangement is R (1), T (2), S (3), Q (4), P (5). Between Q on floor 4 and R on floor 1 live T and S, that is two persons."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A and B together can finish a job in 12 days, B and C together in 15 days, and C and A together in 20 days. In how many days can A alone finish the job?", ["24 days", "30 days", "40 days", "20 days"], 1, "Adding the three pairs: 2(A + B + C) = 1/12 + 1/15 + 1/20 = 1/5, so A + B + C = 1/10 per day. A = 1/10 − (B + C) = 1/10 − 1/15 = 1/30. A alone takes 30 days."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The table shows total deposits (₹ crore) of a bank branch.\n2021 – 420, 2022 – 480, 2023 – 552, 2024 – 600\nWhat is the percentage increase in deposits from 2022 to 2023?", ["12%", "18%", "15%", "13.5%"], 2, "Increase = 552 − 480 = 72. Percentage increase = 72 ÷ 480 × 100 = 15%. Dividing by the later year's value (552) gives about 13% and is a common mistake; always divide by the base year."]]};
